function chapterRoute(u,i){if(u.n===4&&i<10)return ["#unit4-design","#unit4-sql","#unit4-project","#unit4-analysis","#unit4-controls","#unit4-crypto","#unit4-ethics","#unit4-review","#unit4-consolidation","#unit4-exam"][i];if(u.n===3&&i<14)return ["#unit3-fluency","#unit3-structures","#unit3-search","#unit3-sort","#unit3-objects","#unit3-inheritance","#unit3-project","#unit3-reliability","#unit3-network-models","#unit3-addressing","#unit3-secure-networks","#unit3-review","#unit3-consolidation","#unit3-exam"][i];if(u.n===2&&i<10)return '#'+['database-foundations','entities-relationships','normalisation','sql-basics','database-solution','security-threats','responsible-data','unit2-review','unit2-consolidation','unit2-exam'][i];return u.n===1&&i<16?'#'+['start','values','representation','selection','loops','functions','arrays','files','design','software-responsibility','networks','addressing','network-design','network-review','consolidation','exam-preparation'][i]:`#plan-${u.n}-${i}`}
function cards(){return '<div class="grid">'+units.map(u=>`<section class="card" id="unit${u.n}"><span class="eyebrow">YEAR ${u.year} / UNIT ${u.n}</span><h2>${u.title}</h2><p>${u.desc}</p>${u.topics.map((t,i)=>`<a class="chapter" href="${chapterRoute(u,i)}">${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} &nbsp; ${t}<small>${(u.n===4&&i<10)||(u.n===1&&i<16)||(u.n===2&&i<10)||(u.n===3&&i<14)?'Draft':'Planned'}</small></a>`).join('')}</section>`).join('')+'</div>'}
function selection(){return `<article class="lesson"><p class="eyebrow">UNIT 1 / PROGRAMMING / WEEK 3 / CHAPTER 4</p><h1>Making decisions<br>with selection.</h1><p class="lede">A program can make a choice by checking a condition. In this chapter, you will use an order total to decide whether delivery is free.</p><span class="pill">PYTHON 3</span> <span class="pill">DRAFT · TEACHER REVIEW PENDING</span><div class="no-print" style="margin-top:20px"><button id="print">Print / save as PDF</button> <button id="download">Download Python example</button></div><h2>What you’ll learn</h2><div class="objectives"><ul><li>Explain how selection differs from sequence.</li><li>Use a comparison to produce a Boolean result.</li><li>Write an <code>if / else</code> decision in Python.</li><li>Test a decision just below, at and above its boundary.</li></ul></div>${renderLessonPreparation('selection')}<h2>1. From an instruction to a decision</h2><p>A school club offers free delivery for orders of $50 or more. For smaller orders, delivery costs $5. The program must choose between these two charges. Choosing which instructions to run is called <strong>selection</strong>.</p><p>Ask: <em>is the order total at least $50?</em> If the condition is true, set delivery to $0. Otherwise, set it to $5. Each possible path is a <strong>branch</strong>. Only one of these two branches runs.</p><div class="steps" aria-label="Decision process"><span>Read the order total</span><span>Test total ≥ 50</span><span>Choose the delivery charge</span></div><h2>2. Design before coding</h2><p>This illustrative pseudocode expresses the logic without Python syntax:</p><pre>INPUT order_total\nIF order_total &gt;= 50 THEN\n    delivery_charge = 0\nELSE\n    delivery_charge = 5\nEND IF\nOUTPUT delivery_charge</pre><p>Before you code, state what input the example accepts. Here, assume the total is a number that is zero or greater. This example focuses on choosing the delivery charge. A complete application would also need to handle unsuitable input.</p><h2>3. Implement the decision in Python</h2><pre><code>order_total = float(input("Order total ($): "))\n\nif order_total &gt;= 50:\n    delivery_charge = 0\nelse:\n    delivery_charge = 5\n\nprint(f"Delivery charge: &#36;{delivery_charge:.2f}")</code></pre><p><code>&gt;=</code> compares two values and returns a Boolean. The colon introduces the branch’s body; indentation groups its statements. The final <code>print</code> is outside both branches, so it runs whichever branch was chosen.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Watch the boundary.</strong> “At least $50” includes exactly $50. Using <code>&gt;</code> would incorrectly charge delivery on a $50 order. Also, <code>=</code> assigns a value; <code>==</code> compares values.</div><h2>Combine conditions and choices</h2><p>Use <code>and</code> when both conditions must hold, <code>or</code> when either can hold, and <code>not</code> to reverse a Boolean. Python evaluates <code>not</code> before <code>and</code>, then <code>or</code>; parentheses can make your intended grouping clear. An eligibility rule might be <code>age &gt;= 16 and has_permission</code>.</p><pre>if score &gt;= 80:\n    band = "High"\nelif score &gt;= 50:\n    band = "Pass"\nelse:\n    band = "Developing"</pre><p>Python checks the conditions from top to bottom. It runs the first branch whose condition is true, then skips the rest. If neither condition is true, it runs the else branch.</p><p>Test 49, 50, 79 and 80. The expected bands are Developing, Pass, Pass and High. Keep the 80 check first. If you checked <code>score &gt;= 50</code> first, a score of 80 would enter that branch and never reach the High branch.</p>${renderLearningGuide('selection')}<h2>4. Trace it yourself</h2><p>Change the input below to explore this fixed example. This visualiser traces the decision; it does not execute arbitrary Python.</p><div class="card no-print"><label for="total">Order total ($, non-negative)</label><input id="total" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="50"><p id="trace" aria-live="polite"></p></div><h2>5. Test the boundary</h2><p>Choose tests that exercise both branches, with special attention to the point where the result changes.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Order total</th><th>Condition result</th><th>Expected charge</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$49.99</td><td>False</td><td>$5.00</td></tr><tr><td>$50.00</td><td>True</td><td>$0.00</td></tr><tr><td>$50.01</td><td>True</td><td>$0.00</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These three tests show why <code>&gt;=</code> matters: <code>&gt;</code> would give the wrong charge at exactly $50. Also try everyday amounts such as $10 and $100. Checking unsuitable text or negative totals is an input-validation extension.</p><h2>6. Practice</h2><details><summary>Predict: what happens for an order worth $35?</summary><p><code>35 &gt;= 50</code> is false, so the else branch sets the charge to 5. The output is <code>Delivery charge: $5.00</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Debug: a $50 order is charged delivery. What might be wrong?</summary><p>The comparison may be <code>order_total &gt; 50</code>. Change it to <code>order_total &gt;= 50</code> and run the three boundary tests again.</p></details><details><summary>Apply: a museum charges $8 for ages under 16 and $12 otherwise. Design and test the decision.</summary><pre>if age &lt; 16:\n    ticket_price = 8\nelse:\n    ticket_price = 12</pre><p>Test ages 15, 16 and 17. Expected prices are $8, $12 and $12 respectively. Assume age is a valid non-negative integer.</p></details><div class="card"><h3>Quick check</h3><p>Which comparison correctly represents “at least 50”?</p><div id="quiz"><button data-answer="wrong">total &gt; 50</button> <button data-answer="right">total &gt;= 50</button> <button data-answer="wrong">total == 50</button></div><p id="quiz-feedback" class="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p></div><h2>Chapter recap</h2><p>Use a condition to choose which branch runs. Indent the instructions belonging to each branch. Check both paths, then test just below, at and above the value where the decision changes.</p><details><summary>Teaching notes & syllabus reference</summary><p>This draft addresses part of Unit 1 programming: selection, comparisons and boundary testing. The separate loops chapter extends this lesson into iteration. Suggested lesson: predict the result, trace three inputs, implement the example, then change the threshold and test again.</p><p>Consult the current <a href="${source}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SCSA syllabus and additional support booklet</a> for course requirements and pseudocode conventions. Review the example and terminology before classroom use.</p></details></article>`}
