function renderLegacy(){let route=currentTextbookRoute(),html='';if(route==='selection')html=selection();else if(route==='glossary')html=glossaryPage();else if(route==='sources')html=`<p class="eyebrow">ABOUT THIS EDITION</p><h1>Built for the classroom.</h1><p class="lede">An evolving online textbook for teaching and learning Computer Science ATAR in Western Australia.</p><p class="notice"><strong>2027 teaching draft · edition 0.49.</strong> Source-list check: 30 October 2026. Chapter/download authoring dates and historical audits remain separate.</p><h2>Editorial status</h2><p>This public working edition contains 50 original draft chapters: 16 in Unit 1, 10 in Unit 2, 14 in Unit 3 and 10 in Unit 4. All four units include review and exam preparation. Unit 4 covers relational design, advanced SQL, database development, security analysis, controls, cryptography, ethics, integrated review, full-course consolidation and exam preparation.</p><p><a href="#audits">Coverage &amp; audits</a> contains the four editorial maps and Unit 3 convention/practical evidence clinic. These locate written resources; they do not certify alignment or learner achievement. Independent teacher review, conventions, fresh learner evidence, source currency and pacing remain pending. Audit editions/dates are historical snapshots; later website updates do not imply new sign-off.</p><h2>Projects, practice and timing</h2><p>Each semester allocates 64 lessons in 16 teaching weeks, with Weeks 17–18 blocked for exams. Projects, practice, feedback and revision are included. Unit 3 uses 12 embedded supervised project lessons; Unit 4 uses four foundations and 16 embedded workshops. Adapt the suggested sequence to your classroom contact hours.</p><p>Starters are deliberately unfinished. Reference solutions illustrate limited classroom behaviour with synthetic data, not production deployment evidence. Practice papers are original formative samples, not official full examinations or grade predictions. Worksheets, practice questions, quizzes and explained answers are open learning resources. The website does not grade, collect submissions or track students.</p><p><a href="resource-edition.md" download>Download resource edition and review status</a> · <a href="#teaching">Teaching sequence</a>.</p><h2>Curriculum reference</h2><p>Teaching target: <strong>2027</strong>. Year 11 uses the syllabus <strong>for teaching from 2026</strong>; Year 12 uses the syllabus <strong>for teaching from 2027</strong>, as currently listed on the <a href="${source}">official SCSA Computer Science course page</a> (checked 30 October 2026). <a href="curriculum/">The current item-level matrix</a> records source locators, suggested evidence and unresolved gaps. It does not establish complete alignment or replace school review. The 2026 Year 12 syllabus is a historical basis, not this edition’s target. Official documents remain the authority for examinable content. This book is independent and is not endorsed by SCSA.</p><h2>Sharing and ownership</h2><p>This textbook is free to read without an account. Original content is licensed under <a rel="license" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>; original software and runnable example code use the MIT licence. See <a href="licence.html">reuse and attribution details</a>. Third-party resources retain their own terms. <a href="https://github.com/EdugenIO/cs-atar-textbook">Browse or fork the source on GitHub</a>.</p><h2>How the book will grow</h2><p>Draft → teacher review → classroom trial → revision → release. Each chapter should include explanations, examples, practical tasks, explained answers and source records.</p>`;else if(route.startsWith('plan-')){let [,n,i]=route.split('-'),u=units.find(x=>x.n===Number(n)),t=u?.topics[Number(i)];html=t?`<p class="eyebrow">UNIT ${n} / PLANNED CHAPTER</p><h1>${t}</h1><div class="notice">This chapter is on the writing plan. Teaching content has not been written or reviewed yet.</div><h2>Chapter authoring checklist</h2><ul><li>Map learning objectives to current syllabus requirements.</li><li>Write an explanation and worked example.</li><li>Add a practical activity and explained practice answers.</li><li>Check accessibility, sources and permissions.</li><li>Review with the teacher, then trial in class.</li></ul><a class="button" href="#contents">Back to contents</a>`:'<h1>Chapter not found</h1><a href="#contents">View contents</a>';}else if(route==='contents')html='<p class="eyebrow">THE TEACHING PLAN</p><h1>Four units.<br>One place to learn.</h1><p class="lede">Fifty classroom draft chapters span Units 1–4. Use Coverage & audits to inspect the evidence, practice gaps and outstanding review before classroom release.</p><p><a class="button" href="resources/">Find lesson resources</a> <a class="button secondary" href="activities/programming-foundations/">Programming foundations: printable activity pack</a></p>'+cards();else html=textbookHome();document.querySelector('main').innerHTML=html+footer;window.scrollTo(0,0);if(route==='selection'){document.querySelector('#download').onclick=()=>{let blob=new Blob(['order_total = float(input("Order total ($): "))\n\nif order_total >= 50:\n    delivery_charge = 0\nelse:\n    delivery_charge = 5\n\nprint(f"Delivery charge: ${delivery_charge:.2f}")\n'],{type:'text/plain'}),a=document.createElement('a');a.href=URL.createObjectURL(blob);a.download='selection_example.py';a.click();setTimeout(()=>URL.revokeObjectURL(a.href),1000)};let input=document.querySelector('#total'),trace=()=>{let v=input.valueAsNumber;document.querySelector('#trace').textContent=!Number.isFinite(v)||v<0?'Enter a non-negative amount.':`${v.toFixed(2)} ≥ 50 is ${v>=50?'True':'False'} → ${v>=50?'if':'else'} branch → delivery charge $${v>=50?'0.00':'5.00'}.`};input.oninput=trace;trace();document.querySelectorAll('[data-answer]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>document.querySelector('#quiz-feedback').textContent=b.dataset.answer==='right'?'Correct. Greater than or equal to includes the boundary value.':'Try again. The comparison must include 50 and every value above it.');}}
